it is one of the biggest climate threats  that the uk  faces and new research has found that  flooding also has a direct impact  on our health in pentra in shropshire  this house has been marooned three times  in 11 months  it's taken its toll on its owner it's  physically hard work it's utterly  exhausting  if one knows that the water is coming  you lift everything and i did this year  um in the days before the flood i got  all my furniture on trestles i  almost everything was safe from the  water but then it goes  and i'm left with a lot of muck in the  house a lot of damage to the house  and i'm physically drained the report  found that around 1.8 million people in  the uk  are living in areas at significant risk  of flooding  that number could increase to 2.6  million  in as little as 17 years and flood  victims are four  times more likely to suffer mental  health problems  than those unaffected by flooding  when it comes to extreme hot weather  researchers said in 2018  a series of heat waves made more likely  by  climate change led to eight and a half  thousand deaths  but they say something can be done we  need to tackle climate change  um the kind of root cause of it and and  we need to do that  by decarbonizing our economy making sure  that our homes are greener our transport  sector is greener  and actually that brings with it lots of  co-benefits so we know that  um people who walk and cycle more are  healthier um  you know and less of a burden on the nhs  um but also  that that brings with it benefits in  terms of cleaner air that  that helps people um right across the  country last year was the hottest on  record  and it's estimated that 12 million  people in the uk  will be vulnerable to heat waves in  future  researchers say the sooner we can  achieve a cleaner  greener world the better it will be for  our health  lisa dale sky news
 

